                                             
        
       Agentic Development Report 
       This report should be written at a high level, targeted toward developers familiar with your 
       project. You can assume the reader is familiar with programming topics and with your 
       project but may not have exhaustive knowledge of all areas of the same. 
       This is a technical report, and no formal academic referencing is required. Clearly state 
       each group member’s full name, student ID number, and student email address on the 
       first page. Divide the report into sections for each of the points listed below. The expected 
       length is 6-10 pages of text, excluding front-matter, headers, figures, appendices, and 
       tables; though this will vary if there are many or few items to report on. 
       Submit the report as a PDF document through moodle prior to the due date listed on the 
       moodle  submission  portal.  Late  submissions  will  not  be  accepted,  please  upload 
       regular  draft  versions  of  your  report  (uploaded  without  clicking  the  final  submit  for 
       grading button) to ensure that you do not miss the submission window. Only one member 
       of your group needs to submit the report. 
       Individual grades will be derived from the group grade, weighted by the peer review 
       completed by other group members. 
        
        
        
        
        
        
        
        
        
        
        
        
                                             
        
                                                                                                                        
                  
                  
                 Task Description 
                 Using agentic development tools, attempt a large-scale rewrite of the open source 
                 project you have been contributing to, and evaluate which workflows were effective for a 
                 change of that size. Up to this point your work has been scoped to individual issues: small, 
                 well-bounded changes that fit comfortably within a single session. This assessment asks 
                 you to deliberately work at a scale where that no longer holds, and to find out what 
                 changes when it doesn’t. 
                 There is no expectation that you will complete the rewrite. You are assessed on the 
                 quality of your experimentation and evaluation, not on how much of the codebase you 
                 converted. 
                  
                 Choosing a rewrite 
                 Your  rewrite  must  be  system-wide:  it  should  touch  a  substantial  proportion  of  the 
                 codebase and cannot be completable as a sequence of independent, file-local edits. 
                 Suitable examples include: 
                     •   Migrating a backend or library from JavaScript to TypeScript (with meaningful 
                         types, not blanket any). 
                     •   Converting a frontend from one framework to another, such as React to Vue or 
                         Angular to React. 
                     •   Replacing a core dependency that the codebase is structured around, such as an 
                         ORM, state-management library, or HTTP framework. 
                     •   Crossing a major version boundary with widespread breaking changes, such as a 
                         framework upgrade that requires architectural changes rather than find-and-
                         replace. 
                     •   Replacing the build, test, or packaging toolchain where the change reaches into 
                         most modules. 
                 Discuss your proposed changes with your lecturer at your usual meeting time, or via 
                 email to ensure you have chosen something reasonable. 
                  
                 Working environment 
                     •   Work on a fork or a dedicated branch. Your work is not expected to be merged 
                         upstream. Do not open pull requests against the upstream for this work. 
                                                                                                                        
                  
                                                                                                                        
                  
                     •   Establish a baseline before you start: record whether the project builds, the state 
                         of the test suite (pass/fail counts and coverage where available), and any other 
                         measures you will use to judge progress. 
                     •   Run  agents  in  a  sandboxed  environment.  Do  not  give  them  credentials  for 
                         upstream repositories, package registries, or production services, and review 
                         what permissions each tool is granted. 
                 Agentic tools 
                 You may use any agentic coding tool, such as Claude Code, OpenAI Codex, Gemini CLI, 
                 Cursor’s agent mode, or Aider. You may use more than one tool, and comparing tools on 
                 the same task is encouraged but not required. 
                  
                 Workflows 
                 You must attempt at least three meaningfully different workflows to approach the rewrite. 
                 Workflows differ in how the problem is decomposed, how context is given to the agent, 
                 and how results are verified. Rewording the same prompt is not a different workflow. 
                 Approaches you might compare include: 
                     •   A single high-level instruction to convert the whole project, as a baseline. 
                     •   Having the agent produce a written migration plan first, reviewing it, and then 
                         executing it in stages. 
                     •   Incremental, module-by-module conversion using the existing test suite as the 
                         acceptance check at each step. 
                     •   Maintaining  a  project  context  file  (e.g.  CLAUDE.md  or  AGENTS.md)  with 
                         conventions, decisions, and progress that persists across sessions. 
                     •   Running  multiple  agents  or  sub-agents  in  parallel  on  separate  parts  of  the 
                         codebase. 
                     •   Incremental coexistence approaches where old and new code run side by side 
                         while the system is migrated piece by piece. 
                 You are free to design your own workflows; the list above is illustrative rather than 
                 prescriptive. 
                  
                 Evidence log 
                 Keep a record as you work. Your report will be marked on claims you can support. At a 
                 minimum, retain: 
                     •   Session transcripts or exported logs for each workflow, including your prompts. 
                     •   Any plan documents, context files, or configuration you gave to the agent. 
                                                                                                                        
                  
                                                                                                                        
                  
                     •   Frequent commits, with messages that make clear which workflow and session 
                         produced them. 
                     •   Build and test results at key points, so progress and regressions can be discussed. 
                     •   Approximate time spent, and token usage or cost where the tool reports it. 
                  
                  
                 Report Content 
                 Produce a critical evaluation of how you approached a large-scale rewrite with agentic 
                 tools, what worked, what did not, and why. This is not a tool review or a diary of your 
                 sessions: you are forming an argued judgement about which workflows are effective for 
                 large-scale  change,  grounded  in  evidence  from  your  own  attempts  and  in  the 
                 characteristics of the codebase you were working in. 
                 This report has been divided into sections, described below. 
                     •   Scope and Baseline (3 points). Briefly establish the context for your evaluation: 
                         how the project is architected at a high level, the rewrite you attempted and why 
                         it is system-wide, the tools you used, and the baseline state of the project before 
                         you started. State how you measured progress and summarise honestly how far 
                         you got. 
                          
                                 Marking Notes: You have already covered the structure in detail in earlier 
                                 reports, so keep this part brief. This section is just grounding the rest of the 
                                 report  and  helps  for  framing  the  later  sections.  I  want  to  know  what 
                                 “progress” means for your rewrite and what you measured it against. A 
                                 clear statement such as “we converted 14 of 62 modules; the build passes 
                                 but 23 of 410 tests fail, compared with 0 at baseline” is the sort of thing I’m 
                                 looking for. 
                                  
                     •   Workflows and Evaluation (12 points). Describe and evaluate at least three 
                         distinct workflows you used. For each workflow, explain what the approach was 
                         in  concrete  terms:  how  you  decomposed  the  problem,  what  context  and 
                         instructions you gave the agent, and how you verified its output. Then explain what 
                         happened,  supported  by  evidence  from  your  log.  Explain  why  you  think  it 
                         produced  those  results,  identifying  the  underlying  cause  rather  than  the 
                         symptom; and what you would change if you ran it again. 
                          
                                                                                                                        
                  
                                                                                                                        
                  
                                 Marking Notes: Workflows should be genuinely different in structure, not 
                                 variations in prompt wording. The strongest answers explain causes: “the 
                                 agent  introduced  inconsistent  type  names  across  modules”  is  an 
                                 observation; showing that this happened because each session started 
                                 without  knowledge  of  decisions  made  in  earlier  sessions,  and  that  a 
                                 persistent context file reduced it, is an analysis. I’m not looking for generic 
                                 prompting tips or claims that a tool is “good” or “bad”.  
                                  
                                 Failed workflows are as valuable as successful ones and are often more 
                                 instructive. Consider recurring failure modes where you saw them, such 
                                 as  loss  of  context  over  long  sessions,  hallucinated  APIs,  tests  being 
                                 weakened or deleted to make them pass, premature claims of completion, 
                                 and drift from project conventions, along with what you did to detect and 
                                 counter them.  
                                  
                                 Every claim should be locatable in your evidence via link to commits, 
                                 transcripts,  or  log  excerpts  that  support  it.  Attach  your  evidence  as 
                                 appendices, referring to them as needed. 
                                  
                     •   Codebase Factors (5 points). Identify properties of the project itself that made 
                         agentic rewriting easier or harder. For each, explain the mechanism by which it 
                         affected the agent’s behaviour or your ability to verify its work, referring to specific 
                         files, modules, or tooling in the repository. 
                          
                                 Marking Notes: Draw on what you learned about the codebase in earlier 
                                 assessments.  Relevant  factors  might  include  test  coverage  of  critical 
                                 paths,  the  clarity  of  module  boundaries  and  coupling  between 
                                 components,  consistency  of  conventions,  the  quality  of  existing  type 
                                 information or documentation, build and CI speed, and how much implicit 
                                 knowledge the code depends on.  
                                  
                                 “The project has tests, which helped” is an observation. I’m looking for 
                                 specific links between a property of the code and a result you saw, for 
                                 example showing that modules with high coupling to a shared global state 
                                 were consistently where the agent’s conversions broke. 
                                  
                     •   Synthesis and Recommendations (5 points). If another team were about to 
                         attempt a comparable rewrite on a comparable project using agentic tools, what 
                         workflow would you recommend they use, and why? Justify your recommendation 
                         by weighing what you observed against cost, time, risk, and the effort of human 
                         review. Identify where in the process human judgement was needed and helpful, 
                                                                                                                        
                  
                                                                                                                        
                  
                         and where it could safely be delegated. Finally, identify the circumstances under 
                         which you would advise against using agentic tools for a change of this kind at all. 
                          
                                 Marking Notes: This section marks judgement, not coverage, and it should 
                                 not restate the workflows described earlier. I’m looking for evidence that 
                                 you  can  distinguish  what  generalises  from  what  was  specific  to  your 
                                 project or tool, and that you recognise that a workflow producing a lot of 
                                 code quickly is not automatically a good one if its output is costly to verify.  
                                  
                                 Consider the cost of review for the humans who would have to accept this 
                                 work, including open source maintainers. Recognising the limits of your 
                                 own evidence, such as a single project, a small number of runs, or a single 
                                 tool, is part of a strong answer. 
                                  
                     •   Contribution summary (5 points). Provide a summary of your group contributions 
                         to your project over the semester. You should not include your agentic rewrite 
                         tasks in this section. This should include all tasks that you attempted, even if they 
                         were abandoned For each item that has been provide a summary title of the 
                         feature, a list of group members who worked on the feature, links to the relevant 
                         issue(s) on the project issue tracker, and a link to your repository and branch 
                         where the code addressing the issue can be found. If a pull request was opened, 
                         provide a link for this as well. Summarise the issue and your approach(es) to 
                         solving  them,  clearly  identifying  if  discussion/consultation  was  had  with  the 
                         project maintainers or greater project community while developing your solution. 
                         If the change was accepted/rejected by the project maintainers, or if development 
                         was abandoned, provide a brief explanation/justification as to why. An example 
                         of what each of these items might look like is shown below. 
                      
                                 Marking Notes: I’m looking to see an honest accounting of the tasks you 
                                 accomplished this semester while working on your project. The relative 
                                 size or workload associated with each task is not the critical component 
                                 here, but instead being able to account for the work done and backing up 
                                 the decisions you made.  
                  
                                                                                                                        
                  
                                                                                                                        
                  
                 Example contribution summary entry 
                  
                 Feature [ Cameron ]: Add “Scroll to Chapter” function in dual-pane view  
                     •   Issue: https://example.com/user/project/issues/507 - Preview always refreshes 
                         to top-of-document when re-rendering code 
                     •   Repo: https://example.com/my-user/project/tree/feat-507 
                     •   Pull Request: https://example.com/user/project/pull/1022 - Add “Scroll to active 
                         chapter” feature for preview window 
                 When editing a LaTeX document in the code pane, the preview pane live-reloads to 
                 document changes, but it doesn’t automatically scroll to the position in the preview 
                 where you have made your change. Ideally, we would scroll directly to the edit position 
                 in the preview, but calculating this position is tricky and inaccurate due to document 
                 reflowing stages. We can however easily detect which chapter, section, or subsection of 
                 a  document  was  edited  and  scroll  to  that  position.  This  change  was  accepted  on 
                 1970/01/01, with the requester and maintainers being satisfied with the compromise. 
                 There is a wish to further investigate more accurate position tracking so the scrolling can 
                 be more precise, and this has been documented under a new feature request (#562) and 
                 added to the v4 roadmap as a stretch goal.  
                                                                                                                        
                  
